Chapter 8 – The Birth of the 'tech rider' 
Curtis stood beside the red Softail for a moment longer, keys in hand.
He pulled the red Jumpman cap off first, then the gold-framed Louis Vuitton sunglasses, and tucked both carefully into the duffle bag. Without them, the long black braids fell freely down the front of the gray-and-red jacket. He zipped the bag shut and secured it.
Then he reached for the new helmet.
The crimson-shell version settled over his head with a solid, expensive weight. The white angular accents framed the sides. The circular visor glowed a low, steady red as it powered on. The modular braids—red this time—hung down past his shoulders, moving slightly as he adjusted the fit. When the seals locked, the outside world dropped to a muted, focused quiet.
Curtis swung a leg over the Softail and settled into the seat like he had been riding for years. The engine turned over with a deep, rolling growl that vibrated up through the frame. He gave the throttle a single testing blip, listened to the Vance & Hines exhaust answer, and nodded once to himself.
The salesman stood near the door and watched him pull out of the lot.
Curtis didn’t look back.
He leaned into the first turn, the red Harley answering cleanly, and merged into the evening traffic of Ikebukuro with the glowing red visor cutting through the neon. For the first time since he had arrived in the city, the road ahead felt like it belonged to him.
The new bike carried him forward—fast, loud, and finally his.
Night settled over Ikebukuro the way it always did—neon first, then the deeper shadows between the buildings.
Curtis took the long way. He let the red Softail roll through the main avenues and then down narrower side streets, learning the city’s rhythm with the engine’s low growl underneath him. The new helmet kept the wind and the noise at a manageable distance. The glowing red visor painted the road ahead in clean lines. For a while he simply rode, no destination, no one asking questions, nothing required of him except to stay on the bike and keep moving.
It was the most peace he had felt since landing in Japan.
At a red light in a quieter stretch near the edge of the shopping district, a familiar black shape rolled up beside him.
Celty.
The black motorcycle idled smoothly. The yellow cat-eared helmet turned just enough to take in the red Softail and the rider wearing the angular, glowing-visored helmet with the trailing red braids. She didn’t type anything. She simply looked.
Inside the quiet of her own helmet, her thoughts moved carefully.
New bike. Expensive. Custom helmet I’ve never seen on these streets before.
Rides like someone who’s been doing it longer than most people in this city.
Not one of the usual night crowds.
She studied the figure for another second. The jacket was sharp. The posture on the bike was relaxed but ready. Nothing about the silhouette matched the bloodied, closed-off foreigner who had walked out of Shinra’s apartment hours earlier—or the masked man from the night before.
Just another rider.
The light turned green.
Celty stayed beside him for half a block longer than necessary, still quietly assessing, before she finally leaned into a turn and drifted away into the darker streets she knew better than anyone.
Curtis watched the black motorcycle go in his peripheral vision, the glowing red visor tracking her for a moment, then returned his attention to the road.
He didn’t follow.
Not yet.
Curtis watched the black motorcycle begin to pull ahead and let a quiet thought settle in.
I think I can speak to her the way she does.
He didn’t use the helmet’s comms. He didn’t raise his voice. He simply reached for the same strange open channel that had let him hear her in Shinra’s apartment and on the street the night before.
“Hello.”
The word traveled cleanly across the short distance between them—not through speakers, not through air.
Celty’s posture stiffened on the bike so hard the black motorcycle drifted half a lane before she corrected. The yellow helmet snapped toward him. For a split second the usual perfect control she carried on the road fractured into pure, startled stillness.
She had not typed.
She had not spoken aloud.
And still the voice had landed inside the place where only her own thoughts should have existed.
Curtis didn’t wait for a reply.
He gave the red Softail a roll of throttle and pulled away cleanly, the glowing red visor facing forward as he cut down a side street and disappeared into the deeper neon. The custom braids on the new helmet streamed behind him for a moment before the night took him.
Celty remained at the intersection, engine idling, the empty space beneath the yellow helmet fixed on the road he had taken.
Her real voice—unheard by anyone else in the city—came out sharp with disbelief.
“Who the hell?!”
The black motorcycle stayed where it was for several long seconds before she finally moved again, the question still unanswered and the link already gone.
Night had fully settled by the time Curtis rolled the red Softail into the building’s underground garage. He killed the engine, set the kickstand, and sat there for a moment longer than necessary, listening to the quiet tick of cooling metal. The glowing red visor dimmed as he powered the helmet down.
Upstairs, the two-bedroom flat was exactly as he had left it.
He locked the door behind him, crossed to the closet, and set the new helmet carefully on the top shelf beside the folded gray hoodie and the black mask he hadn’t worn since the morning. The modular red braids hung still in the dark.
Only then did he reach into the inside pocket of Justice’s jacket and pull out a small, worn notepad.
The cover was creased soft from years of handling. He flipped it open to a page marked with a short, practical list written in tight handwriting. One of the lines near the middle was still clean. Curtis drew a single, firm stroke through it.
Get a bike.
He stared at the crossed-out line for a second, then closed the notepad and set it on the nightstand.
